From: AR0234 port <local>
Subject: [PATCH 0006] vin-isp: veto ISP 3DNR when the sensor blanking is too short

1920x1200@120 (and 1080p at the sensor's default 133fps) stream with
"isp0 frame lost! / sunxi_isp_reset:isp0 reset!!!,ISP frame number is 0"
on every frame, i.e. the stream delivers nothing. Same sensor, same link
at 1080p30 / 110fps / 720p120 is clean.

Root cause: the ISP D3D (3DNR) block keeps its reference frame in DDR and
feeds it back while the next frame is processed, so it cannot quite finish
an active line within the sensor's line time. The per-line deficit has to
be absorbed by the sensor's vertical blanking. When the blanking is too
short, D3D misses the line time, the ISP raises FRAME_LOST, resets itself
and the next frame misses too - forever.

Measured on this board (AR0234, 6.8us line time, 1920 px/line, ISP clock
324 MHz), 1920x1200 unless noted:

  fll 1220    20 blank lines  120 fps  ->  136 us : FRAME_LOST every frame
  fll 1331   131 blank lines  110 fps  ->  894 us : clean
  fll 2445  1365 blank lines 1080p @60 ->  9.3 ms : clean
  fll 1220   500 blank lines  720p @120 ->  3.4 ms : clean

With D3D disabled the very same 1920x1200@120 runs at 0 lost frames for
150 s (17861 frames, tools/vfr.c), so the rest of the ISP pipeline has
plenty of margin; it is D3D alone that misses the line time.

Fix: tell the ISP the sensor's frame timing whenever the sensor is
configured (sunxi_isp_note_win_cfg(), called from vin_video.c right after
sunxi_isp_sensor_fps()), compute the vertical blanking as
(vts - height) / (fps * vts) - no sensor specific line rate needed, because
the ISP cannot consume more than one line per line time anyway - and when it
is below d3d_min_vblank_us (module parameter, default 500), veto D3D for
that stream:

  - clear D3D_EN in the ISP load image and drop D3D_UPDATE, both at stream
    start and on every frame sync, so the ping-pong load_para buffers the
    ISP DMA-loads can never carry D3D on even if libisp re-applies its
    tuning table (sunxi_isp_gate_d3d()); clearing the bit alone is not
    enough, the residual D3D/LBC state in the load image also has to go;
  - dump that stale D3D/LBC state by re-using the default register image
    exactly as a resolution change does, on the first frame of such a
    stream (sunxi_isp.c, "else if (isp->d3d_blocked)" branch);
  - safety net in the FRAME_LOST irq handler: if it fires anyway (timing
    was never reported, or the rate was raised after S_FMT), drop D3D for
    the rest of the stream and let the existing reset recover, instead of
    resetting on every frame forever.

When the sensor reports no timing at all (vts == 0 or fps == 0) the tuning
table is left alone rather than guessed at. d3d_min_vblank_us=0 turns the
interlock off entirely (old behaviour); a large value restores D3D for
every mode.

Verified on the board with the module built from this patch:
  - 1920x1200@120 for 150 s: 17861 frames, 119.07 fps, inter-frame gap
    max/median = 1.03, 0 gaps > 1.5x median, /sys/kernel/debug/mpp/vi vi0
    "frame cnt: 17862, lost_cnt: 0, error_cnt: 0", dmesg 0 "frame lost",
    0 sunxi_isp_reset, 0 Oops/IOMMU (tools/vfrrun.sh a1-1200p120-150s)
  - 1920x1200@120 for 120 s driven from the serial console, same counters,
    0 "frame lost" in the very same serial log (analysis/t14/serial-0006.log)
  - counterfactual: d3d_min_vblank_us=0 brings the per-frame FRAME_LOST
    back, restoring 500 fixes it again without a reboot
Fields used (struct sensor_config: vts, fps_fixed) already exist in the
vendor UAPI header, so no header change is needed.

The patch also carries one unrelated one-line change to vin.c that is in the
deployed module: the scaler lookup in vin_create_media_links() used the loop
index i where the rest of the file uses vinc->vipp_sel. It is a no-op on this
SoC (vin.c indexes vind->vinc[] by device_id, which is vipp_sel); it is only
here so that patches/ reproduces the deployed .ko.
---
 vin-video/vin_video.c |  3 +++
 vin-isp/sunxi_isp.c   | 96 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 vin-isp/sunxi_isp.h   | 13 +++++++++++++
 vin.c                 |  2 +-

--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-video/vin_video.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-video/vin_video.c
@@ -1120,6 +1147,9 @@
 #endif
 	if (ret == 0) {
 		sunxi_isp_sensor_fps(cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_ISP], win_cfg.fps_fixed);
+		/* let the ISP veto 3DNR when this mode leaves too little blanking */
+		sunxi_isp_note_win_cfg(cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_ISP], win_cfg.width, win_cfg.height,
+				       win_cfg.vts, win_cfg.fps_fixed);
 #ifdef SUPPORT_ISP_TDM
 		if (cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_TDM_RX])
 			sunxi_tdm_fps_clk(cap->pipe.sd[VIN_IND_TDM_RX], win_cfg.fps_fixed,
--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-isp/sunxi_isp.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-isp/sunxi_isp.c
@@ -68,6 +68,34 @@
 #define MAX_IN_WIDTH			4224
 #define MAX_IN_HEIGHT			4224
 
+/*
+ * 3DNR (the ISP D3D block) keeps its reference frame in DDR and feeds it back
+ * while the next frame is processed, so it cannot quite finish an active line
+ * inside the sensor's line time. The per-line deficit has to be absorbed by the
+ * vertical blanking, and when the blanking is too short the ISP raises
+ * FRAME_LOST on every frame and resets itself until the stream never delivers.
+ *
+ * Measured on the AR0234 (6.8us line time, 1920 pixels per line, ISP clock
+ * 324MHz) at 1920x1200:
+ *   fll 1220   20 blank lines  120 fps ->  136us : FRAME_LOST every frame
+ *   fll 1331  131 blank lines  110 fps ->  894us : clean
+ *   fll 2445 1365 blank lines  1080p @ 60 -> 9.3ms : clean
+ *   fll 1220  500 blank lines  720p @ 120 -> 3.4ms : clean
+ * With 3DNR disabled the very same 1920x1200@120 runs at 0 lost frames over
+ * minutes, so the rest of the ISP pipeline has plenty of margin and it is D3D
+ * alone that misses the line time.
+ *
+ * When the sensor reports less vertical blanking than this, clear D3D_EN in the
+ * ISP load image for that stream instead of letting the pipeline die. 0 turns
+ * the interlock off and always trusts the userspace tuning table.
+ */
+static unsigned int d3d_min_vblank_us = 500;
+module_param(d3d_min_vblank_us, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(d3d_min_vblank_us,
+	"min sensor vertical blanking in us that still allows ISP 3DNR, 0 = no interlock");
+
+static void sunxi_isp_gate_d3d(struct isp_dev *isp);
+
 static struct isp_pix_fmt sunxi_isp_formats[] = {
 	{
 		.fourcc = V4L2_PIX_FMT_SBGGR8,
@@ -842,6 +870,21 @@
 			memcpy(&isp->save_size, &isp->isp_ob.ob_black, sizeof(struct isp_size));
 			isp->load_flag = 0;
 			memcpy(isp->isp_load.vir_addr, &isp_default_reg[0], ISP_LOAD_REG_SIZE);
+		} else if (isp->d3d_blocked) {
+			/*
+			 * 3DNR cannot meet the line time in this mode (see
+			 * d3d_min_vblank_us). The image still in isp_load belongs
+			 * to a stream that ran 3DNR, and clearing D3D_EN alone is
+			 * not enough: the D3D/LBC register side that was armed with
+			 * it keeps the ISP from finishing a line, so the same
+			 * 1920x1200@120 that streams cleanly otherwise raises
+			 * FRAME_LOST on every frame. Reuse the default register
+			 * image exactly as a resolution change does - that state is
+			 * known good - and let libisp re-apply its tuning tables on
+			 * the first frame sync.
+			 */
+			isp->load_flag = 0;
+			memcpy(isp->isp_load.vir_addr, &isp_default_reg[0], ISP_LOAD_REG_SIZE);
 		} else if (isp->large_image == 3) {
 			memcpy(isp->isp_load.vir_addr, &isp_default_reg[0], ISP_LOAD_REG_SIZE);
 		} else if (isp->load_flag) {
@@ -958,6 +1001,10 @@
 		bsp_isp_module_disable(isp->id, D3D_EN);
 		load_val = load_val & ~D3D_UPDATE;
 #else
+		if (isp->d3d_blocked) {
+			sunxi_isp_gate_d3d(isp);
+			load_val = load_val & ~D3D_UPDATE;
+		}
 		bsp_isp_clr_d3d_rec_en(isp->id);
 #endif
 		load_val = load_val | (GAMMA_UPDATE | CEM_UPDATE);
@@ -2857,6 +2904,19 @@
 	if (bsp_isp_get_irq_status(isp->id, FRAME_LOST_PD)) {
 		vin_err("isp%d frame lost!\n", isp->id);
 		bsp_isp_clr_irq_status(isp->id, FRAME_LOST_PD);
+		/*
+		 * Safety net for the case the interlock could not see coming: the
+		 * sensor never reported its timing, or the rate was raised after
+		 * set_fmt. 3DNR is what cannot meet the line time, so drop it for
+		 * the rest of this stream and let the reset below recover instead
+		 * of looping forever (every reset also discards a frame).
+		 */
+		if (!isp->d3d_blocked) {
+			isp->d3d_blocked = 1;
+			isp->d3d_blocked_log = 0;
+			vin_err("isp%d: frame lost with 3DNR on, disabling 3DNR for this stream\n",
+				isp->id);
+		}
 		sunxi_isp_reset(isp);
 		__sunxi_isp_reset_v3(isp);
 	}
@@ -3095,10 +3155,16 @@
 			isp->d3d_rec_reset = 0;
 		}
 
+		/* isp_load now holds this frame's image: keep 3DNR off in it */
+		sunxi_isp_gate_d3d(isp);
+
 		load_val = bsp_isp_load_update_flag(isp->id);
 #if !defined CONFIG_D3D
 		bsp_isp_module_disable(isp->id, D3D_EN);
 		load_val = load_val & ~D3D_UPDATE;
+#else
+		if (isp->d3d_blocked)
+			load_val = load_val & ~D3D_UPDATE;
 #endif
 		load_val = load_val | (GAMMA_UPDATE | CEM_UPDATE);
 		bsp_isp_update_table(isp->id, load_val);
@@ -3489,6 +3555,70 @@
 	isp->h3a_stat.sensor_fps = fps;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Record the sensor's frame timing and decide whether 3DNR can run in it.
+ *
+ * vts is the sensor's FRAME_LENGTH_LINES and fps the rate that fll produces
+ * (GET_CURRENT_WIN_CFG reports both), so the blanking is
+ * (vts - height) / vts of one frame period. Only height, vts and fps are
+ * needed - no sensor specific line rate - because the ISP cannot consume more
+ * than one line per line time anyway; what 3DNR runs out of is time, not
+ * pixels.
+ */
+void sunxi_isp_note_win_cfg(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, unsigned int width,
+			    unsigned int height, unsigned int vts, unsigned int fps)
+{
+	struct isp_dev *isp = v4l2_get_subdevdata(sd);
+	unsigned int vblank_us = 0;
+
+	if (vts && fps && vts > height)
+		vblank_us = (unsigned int)(1000000ULL * (vts - height) /
+					   ((unsigned long long)fps * vts));
+
+	isp->sensor_vts = vts;
+	isp->sensor_height = height;
+	isp->sensor_vblank_us = vblank_us;
+
+	/*
+	 * fps or vts of 0 means the sensor did not report its timing at all:
+	 * leave the tuning table alone rather than guessing. vts == height (so
+	 * vblank_us == 0 with a valid vts) really is zero blanking, and the
+	 * interlock applies.
+	 */
+	if (!d3d_min_vblank_us || !vts || !fps) {
+		isp->d3d_blocked = 0;
+		return;
+	}
+
+	isp->d3d_blocked = vblank_us < d3d_min_vblank_us;
+	isp->d3d_blocked_log = 0;
+	vin_print("isp%d sensor %ux%u vts %u fps %u: vblank %u us, 3DNR %s (limit %u us)\n",
+		  isp->id, width, height, vts, fps, vblank_us,
+		  isp->d3d_blocked ? "off" : "allowed", d3d_min_vblank_us);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Clear D3D_EN in the ISP load image.
+ *
+ * bsp_isp_module_disable() writes into isp->isp_load, and the ping-pong
+ * load_para buffers the ISP DMA-loads every frame are copied from it, so
+ * clearing the bit each frame keeps D3D off no matter what the userspace
+ * tuning table asks for. Call it with isp_load holding the current image,
+ * i.e. after load_shadow has been copied in.
+ */
+static void sunxi_isp_gate_d3d(struct isp_dev *isp)
+{
+	if (!isp->d3d_blocked)
+		return;
+
+	bsp_isp_module_disable(isp->id, D3D_EN);
+	if (!isp->d3d_blocked_log) {
+		isp->d3d_blocked_log = 1;
+		vin_print("isp%d: 3DNR forced off, sensor vblank %u us < %u us\n",
+			  isp->id, isp->sensor_vblank_us, d3d_min_vblank_us);
+	}
+}
+
 void sunxi_isp_debug(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, struct isp_debug_mode *isp_debug)
 {
 	struct isp_dev *isp = v4l2_get_subdevdata(sd);
--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-isp/sunxi_isp.h
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin-isp/sunxi_isp.h
@@ -138,6 +138,15 @@
 	bool d3d_rec_reset;
 	unsigned int d3d_lbc_ratio;
 #endif
+	/*
+	 * Sensor frame timing as reported by GET_CURRENT_WIN_CFG; it drives the
+	 * 3DNR / blanking interlock (see d3d_min_vblank_us in sunxi_isp.c).
+	 */
+	unsigned int sensor_vts;
+	unsigned int sensor_height;
+	unsigned int sensor_vblank_us;
+	int d3d_blocked;		/* 3DNR off for the current stream */
+	int d3d_blocked_log;		/* "3DNR forced off" already logged */
 	struct isp_size err_size;
 	struct isp_size save_size;
 	struct isp_debug_mode isp_dbg;
@@ -214,6 +223,8 @@
 int sunxi_isp_g_parm(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, struct v4l2_streamparm *parms);
 void sunxi_isp_sensor_type(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, int use_isp);
 void sunxi_isp_sensor_fps(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, int fps);
+void sunxi_isp_note_win_cfg(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, unsigned int width, unsigned int height,
+			    unsigned int vts, unsigned int fps);
 void sunxi_isp_debug(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, struct isp_debug_mode *isp_debug);
 void sunxi_isp_ptn(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, unsigned int ptn_type);
 void sunxi_isp_frame_sync_isr(struct v4l2_subdev *sd);
--- a/bsp/drivers/vin/vin.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/vin/vin.c
@@ -2126,7 +2126,7 @@
 		cap_sd = &vinc->vid_cap.subdev;
 
 		/* SCALER */
-		scaler = vind->scaler[i].sd;
+		scaler = vind->scaler[vinc->vipp_sel].sd;
 		if (scaler == NULL)
 			continue;
 		/* Link Vin Core */
